# Public entry points for ZZ Stack.
#
# TWO HOSTNAMES, ONE MACHINE, one deployment:
#
#   165-232-169-165.nip.io           the console — the only dashboard this deployment has
#   api.165-232-169-165.nip.io       the platform gateway — every non-browser client
#
# THIS FILE IS A TEMPLATE, AND `install-caddy.sh` IS THE ONLY THING THAT SHOULD APPLY IT.
# It substitutes the hostname above for the target's own and the upstream below for wherever
# the gateway is actually published, then refuses the install if either literal survived.
# Copying this file onto a host by hand skips both guards.
#
# THE ADDRESS ABOVE IS ZZ-STACK'S OWN, and that is not a detail. It used to read
# `203-0-113-10.nip.io`, which was true of an address this platform no longer uses. The
# literal went stale and the site block went on serving it. Nothing
# here broke, because install-caddy.sh substitutes them; what broke was every reader who
# took the literal for a fact, including the header paragraphs that used to sit here
# describing a two-deployment topology this platform no longer has. An address is a fact
# with an expiry date. If this file's literal ever stops matching what
# `ssh zz-stack 'curl -s ifconfig.me'` answers, the literal is the thing that is wrong.
#
# ONE THING DIFFERS PER HOST: how Caddy reaches the gateway on port 8764. Here it is
# loopback, because the gateway is published on 127.0.0.1. A host that binds it to a
# tailnet address instead needs that address, and install-caddy.sh reads the real one off
# the running container rather than trusting anything written down.
#
# nip.io resolves <anything>.<ip-with-dashes>.nip.io to that IP, so the api
# subdomain needs no DNS record and Caddy issues its certificate automatically.
#
# Why the api. subdomain exists at all: the console's browser front end reaches the gateway
# over the compose network, so that path always worked — but Claude Code, Codex and Hermes
# run on somebody's laptop and need a public route in. Without this host the terminal
# clients cannot connect from outside, and the failure looks like a hang rather than a
# refusal.
#
# After changing this file:
#   1. run deploy/install-caddy.sh — do not copy it by hand
#   2. set GATEWAY_PUBLIC_URL=https://api.165-232-169-165.nip.io in deploy/.env
#      — every generated client package embeds this value, so a stale one ships
#      unreachable URLs to every person who installs
#   3. systemctl reload caddy && docker compose up -d cred-proxy

# An agent turn runs for minutes: tens of sequential tool calls, each one a round trip to a
# building block. Caddy's defaults would sever the response mid-turn and reproduce, at the
# proxy, exactly the stall this front end was chosen to end.
#
# These are GLOBAL OPTIONS. `timeouts` is not a site directive — placed inside a site block it
# is rejected outright ("unrecognized directive: timeouts"), so `systemctl reload caddy`
# fails, the old config stays loaded, and the site keeps proxying wherever it did before. On a
# host being migrated that is the previous front end, which the release has just removed: the
# public URL answers 502 and nothing in the deployment looks wrong.
{
    servers {
        timeouts {
            read_body   5m
            read_header 1m
            write       30m
            idle        30m
        }
    }
}

# NO SITE BLOCK FOR THE BARE DOMAIN, and no /blocks/* routes. Both were removed on
# 2026-09-10 when this became a single personal deployment: the browser front end is gone, so
# nothing listens on 3080, and the stand-in blocks it proxied OAuth for are gone with it.
#
# What remains is what this deployment is: an MCP gateway that terminal clients reach, and a
# console to read the record in. A site block pointing at a port nothing serves is worse than
# no site block — Caddy answers 502 with a valid certificate, which reads as "the app is
# broken" rather than "there is no app here".
api.165-232-169-165.nip.io {

    # THE MOCK BLOCKS' OWN OAUTH ENDPOINTS, reachable from a browser.
    #
    # These stand in for a third-party authorization server, so they are exposed the way a real
    # one is — on a public address of their own, not tunnelled through the gateway. A person's
    # browser has to reach /authorize to give consent; the gateway does discovery and the code
    # exchange server-side, but the consent is the person's to give.
    #
    # ONLY the OAuth paths. /mcp stays private, because the blocks are reached through the
    # gateway so that every call is authorised and recorded, and that must not become optional.
    #
    # `handle` is terminal and Caddy sorts it ahead of a bare `reverse_proxy`, so these match
    # first and everything else still falls through to the gateway below untouched.

    # The gateway authenticates every request itself (Bearer zzp_… resolved
    # against zz.pat), so this proxy adds no authorisation of its own and must
    # not strip the Authorization header.
    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8764 {
        flush_interval -1
        transport http {
            read_timeout 30m
        }
    }

    # A cap on what a client may SEND, not on what it receives — the client
    # package streaming back from /pkg/<client>.tgz is a response and is not
    # bounded here. 20MB is far above any MCP call and far below anything worth
    # buffering, so a runaway body is refused rather than held in memory.
    request_body {
        max_size 20MB
    }
}

# ── ZZ Console: the admin dashboard ──────────────────────────────────────────
# ONE HOST, SPLIT ON PATH, and the split is load-bearing rather than tidy. The
# sign-in cookie the gateway sets is host-only and SameSite=Lax; serving the app
# from one origin and its API from another would make it a third-party cookie
# and every browser would be within its rights to drop it. Same origin also
# means no CORS, no preflight, and nothing for the app to configure.
#
#   /auth/*          the passkey ceremony and logout            → gateway
#   /api/console/*   the read API                                → gateway
#   everything else  the Next app                                → console
165-232-169-165.nip.io {
    handle /auth/* {
        reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8764
    }
    # /oauth/authorize HAS TO LIVE WHERE THE SESSION COOKIE LIVES, and that is here.
    #
    # The gateway is an OAuth authorization server for its own MCP doors, and its authorize
    # endpoint asks one question before anything else: who is at this keyboard. The answer is
    # the `zz_console` cookie, which /auth/* sets — and it is set HOST-ONLY on purpose, so
    # that a subdomain cannot shadow it. That means api.<host> cannot read it.
    #
    # Served from api. it therefore saw no session on every request, sent the person to sign
    # in, and SsoAuth's callback returned them to CONSOLE_PUBLIC_URL — where nothing served
    # /oauth/authorize, so they got the dashboard's 404 with their authorization still
    # unfinished. Here, the cookie is present and the round trip closes.
    #
    # The rest of the flow stays on api.: discovery, /oauth/register and /oauth/token are read
    # by the front end's server, which has no cookie and needs none.
    handle /oauth/* {
        reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8764
    }
    handle /api/console/* {
        # -1 DISABLES RESPONSE BUFFERING, and this path is why it matters here.
        # The discussion thread is delivered as server-sent events, and a
        # buffering proxy holds a stream until the connection closes — so the
        # feature does not fail, it silently never updates, which reads as a bug
        # in the app rather than a line missing from this file. The other two
        # hosts have carried this for the same reason since the front end
        # started streaming; the console host was added later and did not.
        reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8764 {
            flush_interval -1
        }
    }
    handle {
        reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3100
    }
}
